{#
  The infra services eligible for a managed version bump between converges.

  catena-stack-update-managed reads this as its --specs-file. Without it the
  lane runs, finds no specs, and bumps nothing -- which is indistinguishable
  from "nothing needed bumping". Rendered on every converge from either path,
  so a service added here reaches a host that converges itself.

  Every entry is `infra-swarm`, which needs only the service name: the lane
  bumps with `docker service update --image`, so it never has to reproduce how
  the converge built the service and there is no launch argv here to keep in
  step with the role. Services deployed by `docker stack deploy` are ordinary
  swarm services named <stack>_<compose service>, so they take the same path as
  the services the converge creates directly.

  A successful bump records an image pin in /etc/catena/config.json and the next
  converge resolves max(floor, pin). Two readers apply that rule:
  playbooks/filter_plugins/image_pin.py for every entry but cloudflared, and
  catena-admin payload/cmd/catena-cloudflared-sync (imageOrPin) for cloudflared,
  which the converge reconciles through that engine. Adding a service here
  without its image resolving through one of them would mean the lane bumps it
  and the next converge puts it back.

  Client application stacks and the default apps (Gatus, Healthchecks and
  Beszel's hub) are bumped through their folders
  (catena-admin payload/engines/stackupdate) and are not listed here.
  `compose_name` on a stack-deployed entry is what keeps the two apart: the
  engine builds its client-walk skip set from that key, so a stack of Catena's
  own is not also bumped as if a client had chosen it.

  Every entry but cloudflared moves by itself at patch level only: a patch
  release, or for catena-postgres, which numbers its releases major.minor, its
  next minor (its patch release, policy "minor", which keeps the major). Minors
  and majors of the others, and a new Postgres major (a migration: pg_upgrade or
  a dump the restore path can replay), arrive with a catena-admin release. The
  tag pattern of catena-postgres names the major and the trixie variant of the
  tier-1 catalog's PostgresImage (catena-admin
  payload/engines/tier1/catalog.go), and changes with it.

  cloudflared moves to any newer release by itself, after the soak and the
  vulnerability gate: it keeps no data, and an out-of-date tunnel is the larger
  risk. The move and its rollback run on the server and need no tunnel; the
  connector's own readiness, a registered connection to the edge, decides
  whether the move stands.

  The services this manifest leaves out, each with its reason:

    catena-admin      The panel cannot roll itself with a plain service update:
                      the payload has to be reinstalled from the new image in
                      the same operation, or the host runs new engines under an
                      old shell. It has its own lane.
    oauth2-proxy      One stack with one service per gated app, so the set is
                      per-host rather than fixed. They share a repository and
                      therefore one pin, so a converge moves them together
                      after any one of them is bumped.
    beszel-hc-shim    Its image is a python base image, not a product release.
#}
[
  {
    "name": "catena-postgres",
    "kind": "infra-swarm",
    "swarm_service": "catena-postgres",
    "policy": "minor",
    "upstream": {
      "source": "dockerhub",
      "repo": "library/postgres",
      "scheme": "partial_line",
      "tag_pattern": "^18\\.\\d+-trixie$"
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "{{ traefik_container_name }}",
    "kind": "infra-swarm",
    "swarm_service": "{{ traefik_container_name }}",
    "policy": "patch",
    "upstream": {
      "source": "github",
      "repo": "traefik/traefik",
      "tag_pattern": "^v\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+$"
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "beszel-agent",
    "kind": "infra-swarm",
    "compose_name": "{{ beszel_agent_compose_name }}",
    "swarm_service": "{{ beszel_agent_compose_name }}_app",
    "policy": "patch",
    "upstream": {
      "source": "dockerhub",
      "repo": "henrygd/beszel-agent",
      "tag_pattern": "^\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+$"
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "keycloak",
    "kind": "infra-swarm",
    "compose_name": "{{ keycloak_compose_name }}",
    "swarm_service": "{{ keycloak_compose_name }}_{{ keycloak_server_service }}",
    "policy": "patch",
    "upstream": {
      "source": "quay",
      "repo": "phasetwo/phasetwo-keycloak",
      "scheme": "build_suffix",
      "tag_pattern": "^\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+$"
    }
  },
  {
    "name": "cloudflared",
    "kind": "infra-swarm",
    "swarm_service": "cloudflared",
    "policy": "major",
    "ready": ["cloudflared", "tunnel", "--metrics", "127.0.0.1:20241", "ready"],
    "upstream": {
      "source": "dockerhub",
      "repo": "cloudflare/cloudflared",
      "tag_pattern": "^\\d{4}\\.\\d{1,2}\\.\\d+$"
    }
  }
]
